      },
      "question": {
        ":question/problem": "Given that $a=\u27e8-2,3,-5\u27e9,$ $b=\u27e82,-3,5\u27e9,$ and $c=\u27e8-1,1,-2\u27e9,$ find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using the definition of the scalar triple product.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-3)\\cdot (-2)-5\\cdot 1)i-(2\\cdot (-2)-5\\cdot (-1))j+(2\\cdot 1-(-3)\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =i-j-k \\\\  & =\u27e81,-1,-1\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\u27e8-2,3,-5\u27e9\\cdot \u27e81,-1,-1\u27e9 \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-1)+(-5)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
