      "question": {
        ":question/problem": "Given that $a=\u27e8-1,3,-5\u27e9,$ $b=\u27e82,-4,4\u27e9,$ and $c=\u27e8-1,3,-5\u27e9,$ find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using the definition of the scalar triple product.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -4 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-4 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -5\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -5\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-4)\\cdot (-5)-4\\cdot 3)i-(2\\cdot (-5)-4\\cdot (-1))j+(2\\cdot 3-(-4)\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =8i+6j+2k \\\\  & =\u27e88,6,2\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\u27e8-1,3,-5\u27e9\\cdot \u27e88,6,2\u27e9 \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 8+3\\cdot 6+(-5)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
