      },
      "question": {
        ":question/problem": "Given that $a=\u27e8-5,-5,1\u27e9,$ $b=\u27e81,-1,-3\u27e9,$ and $c=\u27e85,-3,2\u27e9,$ find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using the definition of the scalar triple product.\n\n$a\\cdot (b\\times c)=$\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 2-(-3)\\cdot (-3))i-(1\\cdot 2-5\\cdot (-3))j+(1\\cdot (-3)-(-1)\\cdot 5)k \\\\  & =-11i-17j+2k \\\\  & =\u27e8-11,-17,2\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\u27e8-5,-5,1\u27e9\\cdot \u27e8-11,-17,2\u27e9 \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot (-11)+(-5)\\cdot (-17)+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =55+85+2 \\\\  & =142\\end{aligned}\n$$",
