      "question": {
        ":question/problem": "Given that $a=\u27e84,-3,-1\u27e9,$ $b=\u27e8-1,-5,-2\u27e9,$ and $c=\u27e8-2,-3,-3\u27e9,$ find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using the definition of the scalar triple product.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & -5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -3 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-5 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-5)\\cdot (-3)-(-2)\\cdot (-3))i-((-1)\\cdot (-3)-(-2)\\cdot (-2))j+((-1)\\cdot (-3)-(-5)\\cdot (-2))k \\\\  & =9i+j-7k \\\\  & =\u27e89,1,-7\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\u27e84,-3,-1\u27e9\\cdot \u27e89,1,-7\u27e9 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 9+(-3)\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-7) \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
